    "q-340112": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340112",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Given the function $y=-3{log}_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{log}_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
